--------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Simulators — separate programs, invoked as subprocesses
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

1.0 Process isolation — why this section is separate

SimCrux does not embed, link, or statically or dynamically bind ANY
simulator. Every driver under `lib/services/simulator/` extends
`ProcessBackedSimulatorDriver`, which spawns the tool as an independent
operating-system process via `dart:io`'s `Process.start` (through the
`ProcessReaper` seam, which retains a kill handle so a hung simulator can
be terminated). SimCrux exchanges only command-line arguments, environment
variables, working-directory paths, and captured stdout/stderr text with
those processes.

There is no `dart:ffi` usage and no `DynamicLibrary` load in SimCrux's own
source. The desktop CMake/Xcode build configurations under `linux/`,
`macos/`, and `windows/` contain only the stock Flutter runner targets — no
simulator library is compiled or linked.

This is a deliberate, documented invariant, not an accident of the current
implementation. `lib/domain/interfaces/simulator_driver_plugin_registry.dart`
states it as a contract on the Pro-tier plugin SDK:

    **Process isolation.** Implementations MUST run each plugin as a
    separate OS subprocess. `dart:ffi`-style in-process loading is
    forbidden by the open-core/Pro contract because it conflates GPL (or
    proprietary) plugin code with the host process and removes the
    crash-isolation guarantee.

`include/simcrux_driver_plugin_abi.md` gives the same rule with its
rationale, license isolation first.

That boundary matters legally as well as architecturally. Icarus Verilog
and GHDL are GPL-licensed; invoking them as separate programs across a
process boundary keeps SimCrux's own Apache-2.0-licensed code cleanly
separated from their copyleft terms. A change that links a simulator into
the SimCrux process would invalidate that analysis.

1.1 Distribution posture

SimCrux **does not distribute any simulator binary**. Simulators are
resolved at run time by `ProcessBackedSimulatorDriver.resolveBinary()`:
the `system` and `bundled` `SimulatorBinarySource` modes both return the
bare executable name (`iverilog`, `vvp`, `verilator`, `ghdl`, `make`), which
`Process.start` resolves against the host `PATH`; the `custom` mode uses an
explicit path the user configures. The `bundled` mode and the
`assets/sim_binaries/<id>/<platform>/` layout referenced in
`lib/domain/models/simulator_binary_config.dart` are forward-looking
documentation — no such directory exists and no simulator binary is
tracked in this repository.

If bundling is activated later, each simulator's own license obligations
attach to that distribution — including, for the GPL simulators, the
obligation to convey or offer the corresponding source of the binary being
distributed. See §9.

1.2 Icarus Verilog

  Component: Icarus Verilog (`iverilog` compiler, `vvp` runtime)
  Role:      Verilog compilation and simulation; driver at
             `lib/services/simulator/icarus_driver.dart`
             (`iverilog -g<gen> -o sim.vvp -s<top> …` then
             `vvp sim.vvp [-fst|-vcd]`)
  License:   GPL-2.0 (GNU General Public License v2.0; the project's
             source headers state "or later")
  Upstream:  https://github.com/steveicarus/iverilog
  Copyright: Stephen Williams and the Icarus Verilog contributors

  Invoked as a separate program; not linked. See §1.0.

1.3 Verilator

  Component: Verilator
  Role:      Cycle-accurate Verilog/SystemVerilog simulation; driver at
             `lib/services/simulator/verilator_driver.dart`
             (`verilator --cc --exe --build …`, then execution of the
             native model Verilator itself built)
  License:   LGPL-3.0-only OR Artistic-2.0 (dual; the licensee chooses)
  Upstream:  https://github.com/verilator/verilator
  Copyright: Wilson Snyder and the Verilator contributors

  Verilator's own README carries
  `SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-3.0-only OR Artistic-2.0`.

  **A nuance worth stating precisely.** Verilator's `--build` step compiles
  a C++ harness (`sim_main.cpp`, emitted by SimCrux's
  `_writeSimMain()` template) together with Verilator's generated model and
  Verilator's own runtime headers into a **separate native executable**
  under `obj_dir/`. SimCrux then runs that executable as an ordinary child
  process. That compile-and-link happens inside Verilator's own build
  invocation, entirely outside SimCrux's Flutter build — no CMakeLists,
  Podfile, or Xcode phase in this repository compiles or links against
  Verilator's runtime, and the SimCrux application binary never links it.
  The produced model executable is a build artifact on the user's machine,
  not something SimCrux distributes.

